-- liquid_split.mm1 — the proof file for mm0/liquid_split.mm0.
--
-- GENERATED by `scripts/liquid-split-mm0 --write`; edit the script, not
-- this file. `mm0-c` checks these proofs against the specification on
-- every run that compiles them, so the statement and its proof cannot
-- disagree. Proof arguments are hypotheses and term arguments are
-- inferred in proof position, which is why the proofs are one premise or
-- two composed by `veq_trans`: 10 theorem(s), no `sorry`.
--
-- Keep this file's declarations in the same order as the `.mm0`: mm0-c
-- matches them in order, and a swap surfaces as an unrelated unify
-- failure elsewhere.

-- liquid_split.mm0 — the split law L11d stages, stated for the MM0 profile.
--
-- GENERATED by `scripts/liquid-split-mm0 --write`; edit the script, not this
-- file. The proof file `liquid_split.mm1` is derived from this text, so the
-- statements and the proofs cannot drift apart.
--
-- WHAT IS STATED. A buffer `b` split at an index `k`, guarded by `ok_split b k`
-- — the numeric profile's `0 <= k <= len b`, named rather than restated here —
-- yields `left b k` and `right b k`. The law says that the two extents sum to
-- `len b`, that the halves are disjoint, that an index inside a half is an index
-- inside `b`, and that a write into one half does not disturb a read from the
-- other. The last of those is the one a split buffer program actually relies on.
--
-- WHAT IS PROVED (10 theorems, 0 sorry):
--
--   split_extent_conserved             the guarded split conserves the extent
--   split_halves_disjoint              the halves' writes cannot alias
--   split_left_index_is_buffer_index   in-bounds in a half ⇒ in-bounds in `b`
--   split_write_left_keeps_right       a write to the left is invisible on the right
--   split_write_right_keeps_left       … and the mirror image
--   split_write_visible_in_half        a write is visible where it wrote
--   split_one_write_per_half           the halves' writes commute (two writes,
--                                      one read, no interference)
--   split_parts_are_nonnegative        an extent is not negative
--   split_left_fits_the_whole          the left extent is within the sum
--   split_conservation_pins_the_bound  … and within `len b` by conservation
--
-- WHAT IS ASSUMED, AND WHY. MM0 has no arithmetic: `Idx` is an uninterpreted
-- sort, `plus`, `le` and `ieq` are uninterpreted terms, and the split's own laws
-- are axioms. Those three — conservation, disjointness, and an in-half index
-- being a buffer index — are exactly the residual premises the guarded split
-- precondition supplies, so what the theorems add is the *composition*: that
-- those premises suffice for a program to write one half and read the other.
-- A reviewer who disagrees with a premise can point at the one axiom and not at
-- a derivation.
--
-- WHAT IS *NOT* PROVED. That the Austral split function conserves extents, that
-- the elaborated model's index satisfies `ok_split`, or that the residual
-- premises hold of any concrete buffer. Those are L11d's Why3 obligations and
-- the statement comparator's subject; this artifact is the exact statement
-- those obligations instantiate, and it is a statement of record, not a
-- certificate about the implementation.
--
-- `veq_trans` is the only bridge between value equalities, so a composition
-- proof reads as a chain of reads; the two numeric laws (`le_zero`,
-- `le_plus_left`) and their bound (`le_sum_bound`) are what let the extent
-- figures be compared at all without an arithmetic theory.

-- theory: self-declared vocabulary — the sorts and terms below are this
-- artifact's whole theory; it imports no rule from `cic.mm0` or `isa.mm0`, so
-- there is no `used=` coverage list to state. What the split law rests on is
-- the axioms in the next section, each named as a residual premise.

provable sort Prop;
delimiter $ ( ) $;

-- ── The profile's vocabulary ─────────────────────────────────────────────
-- `Idx` is the numeric profile's extent/index domain (the Int64 lengths of the
-- Austral side), `Buf` a buffer, `St` a machine state, `Val` a stored value.
-- None is given a structure: the law below is about extents and interference,
-- not about how a buffer or a state is laid out.
sort Idx;
sort Buf;
sort St;
sort Val;

term zero: Idx;
term plus (x y: Idx): Idx;
term len (b: Buf): Idx;
-- The split, and its guard. `take`/`drop` are the two halves; `ok_split` is the
-- precondition the law is guarded by, named so that the theorem statements carry
-- it as a hypothesis instead of restating a numeric comparison in each one.
term take (b: Buf) (k: Idx): Buf;
term drop (b: Buf) (k: Idx): Buf;
term ok_split (b: Buf) (k: Idx): Prop;
term disjoint (b c: Buf): Prop;
term inb (b: Buf) (i: Idx): Prop;
-- Writing a value into a buffer at an index (`wr`), and reading one back (`rd`).
term wr (s: St) (b: Buf) (i: Idx) (v: Val): St;
term rd (s: St) (b: Buf) (i: Idx): Val;
-- Extent equality and order, and value equality. Uninterpreted: the three
-- numeric axioms below are the whole arithmetic this artifact uses.
term ieq (x y: Idx): Prop;
term veq (x y: Val): Prop;
term le (x y: Idx): Prop;

-- The two halves of a split are named here because every theorem below is
-- stated in those names: a reader should be able to see "left" and "right" in
-- the goal without translating back to `take`/`drop` first.
def left (b: Buf) (k: Idx): Buf = $ take b k $;
def right (b: Buf) (k: Idx): Buf = $ drop b k $;

-- ── The residual premises ────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Value equality composes; the split's conservation and disjointness are
-- assumed *of the guarded split*, which is what makes them premises rather than
-- theorems. Everything the law claims about interference is derived from these.
axiom veq_trans (x y z: Val) (h1: $ veq x y $) (h2: $ veq y z $): $ veq x z $;
-- The numeric profile: an extent is non-negative, and a sum is at least its
-- left part. `le_sum_bound` turns a conserved sum into a bound on the whole —
-- the step that makes `split_conservation_pins_the_bound` a theorem about `b`
-- rather than about `plus`.
axiom le_zero (x: Idx): $ le zero x $;
axiom le_plus_left (x y: Idx): $ le x (plus x y) $;
axiom le_sum_bound (x y z: Idx) (h: $ ieq (plus x y) z $): $ le x z $;
-- Extent conservation: the guarded split's two extents add up to the buffer's.
axiom split_conserves (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (h: $ ok_split b k $):
  $ ieq (plus (len (take b k)) (len (drop b k))) (len b) $;
-- Disjointness: the halves' writes cannot alias, which is the premise every
-- interference theorem below consumes.
axiom split_disjoint (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (h: $ ok_split b k $):
  $ disjoint (take b k) (drop b k) $;
-- An index inside a half is an index inside the buffer the half came from.
axiom split_parts_in (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (i: Idx) (h: $ ok_split b k $)
  (j: $ inb (take b k) i $): $ inb b i $;
axiom disj_sym (b c: Buf) (h: $ disjoint b c $): $ disjoint c b $;
-- The two write/read laws the composition rests on: a write is visible where it
-- wrote, and a write into a buffer disjoint from the one being read is not
-- visible at all.
axiom rd_wr_same (s: St) (b: Buf) (i: Idx) (v: Val):
  $ veq (rd (wr s b i v) b i) v $;
axiom rd_wr_disjoint (s: St) (b c: Buf) (i j: Idx) (v: Val)
  (h: $ disjoint b c $):
  $ veq (rd (wr s b i v) c j) (rd s c j) $;

-- ── The law ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Conservation, restated over `left`/`right`: the definitions unfold, so this is
-- the statement a reader of the split law looks for.
pub theorem split_extent_conserved (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (h: $ ok_split b k $):
  $ ieq (plus (len (left b k)) (len (right b k))) (len b) $ = '(split_conserves (h));
-- Disjointness under the same guard, in the same names.
pub theorem split_halves_disjoint (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (h: $ ok_split b k $):
  $ disjoint (left b k) (right b k) $ = '(split_disjoint (h));
pub theorem split_left_index_is_buffer_index (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (i: Idx)
  (h: $ ok_split b k $) (j: $ inb (left b k) i $): $ inb b i $ = '(split_parts_in (h) (j));
-- Interference: writing into the left half leaves a read of the right half
-- exactly as it was. This is the property that makes a split usable — the two
-- halves may be handed to different code without synchronisation — and its
-- mirror image below.
pub theorem split_write_left_keeps_right (s: St) (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (i j: Idx) (v: Val)
  (h: $ ok_split b k $):
  $ veq (rd (wr s (left b k) i v) (right b k) j) (rd s (right b k) j) $ = '(rd_wr_disjoint (split_disjoint (h)));
pub theorem split_write_right_keeps_left (s: St) (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (i j: Idx) (v: Val)
  (h: $ ok_split b k $):
  $ veq (rd (wr s (right b k) i v) (left b k) j) (rd s (left b k) j) $ = '(rd_wr_disjoint (disj_sym (split_disjoint (h))));
-- Visibility inside a half, with no guard: a write is visible where it wrote.
pub theorem split_write_visible_in_half (s: St) (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (i: Idx) (v: Val):
  $ veq (rd (wr s (left b k) i v) (left b k) i) v $ = '(rd_wr_same);
-- The composition: write the right half, write the left half, then read the
-- right half back — the value the first write left is still there. Neither write
-- saw the other, which is the whole reason the split exists.
pub theorem split_one_write_per_half (s: St) (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (i j: Idx)
  (va vb: Val) (h: $ ok_split b k $):
  $ veq (rd (wr (wr s (right b k) j vb) (left b k) i va) (right b k) j) vb $ = '(veq_trans (rd_wr_disjoint (split_disjoint (h))) (rd_wr_same));
-- The numeric-profile side of the same law: both extents are non-negative, the
-- left one is within the sum, and conservation turns that into a bound by the
-- buffer's own extent.
pub theorem split_parts_are_nonnegative (b: Buf) (k: Idx):
  $ le zero (len (left b k)) $ = '(le_zero);
pub theorem split_left_fits_the_whole (b: Buf) (k: Idx):
  $ le (len (left b k)) (plus (len (left b k)) (len (right b k))) $ = '(le_plus_left);
pub theorem split_conservation_pins_the_bound (b: Buf) (k: Idx) (h: $ ok_split b k $):
  $ le (len (left b k)) (len b) $ = '(le_sum_bound (split_conserves (h)));
